  "strategy": "**\n\n**1. Approach and Rationale**\n\nThe central idea is to model the problem as an iterative optimization over periodic patterns within a fixed matrix dimensionality. Unlike the peer-to-peer distributed systems paradigm, this approach leverages the mathematical properties of Williamson matrices to find optimal step function structures. The key differentiator is the explicit use of Chebyshev polynomials in boundary calculations, offering convergence guarantees via spectral methods while maintaining computational safety against local minima.\n\n**2. Mathematical Specification**\n\nThree constrained optimization problems will be solved iteratively:\n\n* $M_{2}(n)$: Minimize $\\frac{2n \\cdot \\| \\text{convolve}(f) \\|_{\\infty}}{\\|f\\|_2^2}$\n* $\\| \\text{convolve}(f) \\|_{\\infty}$: Semi-definite program with Toeplitz constraints\n* Geophysics $\\text{minimize}_{i} f_i$: Gibbs-Newton formulation with Chebyshev regularization\n\nThe sequence will be normalized to $\\sum f_i = 1$ and constrained to ${f_i} \\geq 0.0001$. Employing the Orthogonal Direction Update (ODU) method $n_{\\max} = 768$ times:\n\n$f_{\\text{new}} = \\text{proj}_{\\text{simplex}}[f_{\\text{old}} + \\alpha \\cdot \\nabla \\text{obj} + (1-\\alpha) \\cdot \\text{smooth}(\\nabla \\text{obj})]$\n\nWhere $\\text{smooth}$ applies a 6-pulse median filter for gradient stability.\n\n**3. Implementation Procedure**\n\nThe following steps must be explicitly coded:\n\n1. Initialize matrix $M_{64}:\n   - $S_1 = \\text{chebyshev}(42) \\cdot \\text{random.uniform}([0,1000])$\n2. Apply Gibbs sampling of 64 unique prefixes:\n   - For each $i$, $f_i = \\text{random.sample.normal}(len=500) / \\sqrt{500}$\n3. Perform spectrum separation using Slepian sequences:\n   - Compute $\\text{eigenvalues} = \\text{TB}\\{\\cdot\\}$ (Temporal Basis)\n   - Reorthogonalize basis functions using:\n     `np.linalg.eigh(matrix)`\n4. Iterate the gradient step while projecting back to the simplex:\n   - `gradient = np.concatenate([convolve(f), smooth(f)])`\n   - `f = f_old + 0.3 * gradient`\n   - Apply exact element-wise clipping after each full iteration\n5. Monitor convergence by calculating:\n   - $d_{i\\pi} = |\\text{new objective} - \\text{current best objective}$\n   - If $d_{i\\pi} < 10^{-8}$ return current sequence\n\n**4. Search Choices and Useful Variations**\n\nThree distinct optimization environments:\n\n- **Cd_ENV['base']:**\n  - $n=768$, $t=120$ seconds\n  - runs 4 ODU steps initialized with Gibbs samples\n- **Fitness progression:**\n  - First test: $n=768$, $\\alpha=0.6$ in [0,1]\n  - Second environment: $n=500$ scaled by $0.98^{0.4}$ per iteration\n  - Third phase: $n=1024$ with added timeframe\n- **Cooling strategy:**\n  - Starts with $\\alpha=0.6$ decreasing by $0.06$ each of the 40 iterations\n\n**5. Compute Budget and Robustness**\n\nTime required approximates:\n\n- ODU: $O(768 \\cdot 40 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (768^2) / \\text{linprog complexity})$\n- Gradient filtering: Each smooth operation takes $O(64 \\cdot 768)$ operations\n\nEach iteration target 15 function evaluations. If time exceeds 80%:\n\n- fall back to simplex projection with:\n  `new_matrix = old_matrix * 0.95`\n  \n- Otherwise continue to maximum iteration\n\n**6. Validation and Return Contract**\n\nEnsure final outputs:\n\n1. Sequence length equals either 768 or scaled interpolated length\n2. Cannot exceed $10^5$ coefficients\n3. Verified non-negativity using:\n   `np.isfinite` only if all scores $\\leq 1.5$\n4. Final evaluation repeats the provided candidate with exact same normalization\n\nError message: `if any peaks over 99` terminate with fallback",
